Post-AGI Alpha Hunt =E2=80=94 10 Sep 2026 Executive view

This pass produced four useful additions to the scarcity-migration kernel.
The strongest is *mandatory physical identity infrastructure*: the EU has
now switched on a machine-readable Digital Product Passport registry, with
the first hard compliance cliff on 18 February 2027. This creates a rare
overlap between *permission scarcity* and *world-state consistency*. The
second is *experimental entropy inflation*: AI appears to expand science
into less-studied, more heterogeneous targets, which can increase demand
for bespoke physical validation even when prediction becomes cheap. The
third is *rare-failure telemetry as a negative-data moat*. The fourth is a
theoretical confirmation from NBER that automation in O-ring systems can
increase the value of the remaining bottleneck task rather than merely
substitute it.
1. Mandatory item-level identity becomes physical-world infrastructure
=E2=80=94 *9.5/10
alpha* Thesis

AGI cannot act safely on a physical world represented by ambiguous SKUs,
stale databases and unverifiable product histories. Until now, better
physical identity was mostly an efficiency choice. The EU is turning it
into a legal prerequisite.

On 20 July 2026 the European Commission made the *Digital Product Passport
Registry operational*. It stores unique product identifiers and metadata,
supports API registration, publishes machine-readable semantic models, and
includes verification/logging. The first hard deadline is *18 February 2027=
*,
when covered EV, LMT and industrial batteries must have a battery passport.
The Commission=E2=80=99s August guidance enumerates *71 required battery-pa=
ssport
data points*.

This is more than ESG/compliance plumbing. It is the beginnings of a
standardized *physical-world state API*:

physical object -> persistent ID -> verified attributes -> lifecycle state
-> machine-readable API -> authorized action

That is directly useful to autonomous procurement, repair, recycling,
insurance, resale, logistics, maintenance and robotics.
Tradable exposures

*Avery Dennison (NYSE: AVY)* is the best risk/reward exposure found. It
already combines RFID/NFC/QR physical identifiers, cloud software and DPP
tooling. It launched ReadyDPP in June 2026. Q2 sales grew 10.9%, adjusted
EPS 19.4%; apparel/general-retail Intelligent Labels grew ~10%. At
roughly *$12.9B
market cap and ~16=E2=80=9317x forward earnings*, valuation is far less dem=
anding
than RFID pure plays.

*Impinj (NASDAQ: PI)* is the cleaner endpoint-RFID semiconductor exposure.
Q2 revenue hit a record $108.4M, but at roughly *$5.25B market cap and
~60=E2=80=9370x forward earnings*, much of the identification-growth narrat=
ive is
already priced.

*Zebra (NASDAQ: ZBRA)* is broader but owns scanners, printers, RFID, RTLS
and machine vision. Q2 Asset Visibility & Automation organic sales rose
11.4%.
Why this matters for the kernel

Add:

identity_resolution
physical_state_addressability
mandatory_machine_readability
passport_coverage
state_provenance_depth

And a new edge:

legal_permission_requires_verified_identity
Falsifiers

   - DPP obligations are materially delayed or watered down.
   - Customers treat DPP as cheap QR-code compliance, preventing meaningful
   software/identifier attach rates.
   - Open standards commoditize identifier economics faster than endpoint
   volumes expand.

